<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guaporé River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iténez River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guaporé, called the Iténez in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that forms a long stretch of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Bolivia before joining the Mamoré. In the 18th century it was the frontier between Portuguese Mato Grosso and the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions of Moxos. The Portuguese built their capital Vila Bela and the Fort of Príncipe da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beira">Beira</a> on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guapore-river.jpg" alt="Guaporé River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Spoladore (own work) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_Guapor%C3%A9_em_Pontes_e_Lacerda1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
